+I migrated the plot from Matplotlib’s 3D axes to an Altair 6.1.0 chart by replacing the imperative 3D wireframe/surface rendering with an Altair layered chart built from the input grid data. Since Altair does not support Matplotlib-style 3D surface plotting or colorbars directly, I converted the `points` array into a tabular form and used a heatmap-style layered visualization to preserve the plotted function values while keeping the same function signature and variable names.

+

+```python

+"""3-D benchmarking functions plots.

+"""

+

+import altair as alt

+import numpy as np

+import pandas as pd

+

+

+def plot(

+    points: np.ndarray,

+    title: str = "",

+    subtitle: str = "",

+    style: str = "winter",

+    colorbar: bool = True,

+) -> None:

+    """Plots the surface from a 3-dimensional function.

+

+    Args:

+        points: Points to be plotted with shape equal to (3, n, n).

+        title: Title of the plot.

+        subtitle: Subtitle of the plot.

+        style: Surface's style.

+        colorbar: If colorbar should be used or not.

+

+    """

+

+    x = points[0]

+    y = points[1]

+    z = points[2]

+

+    data = pd.DataFrame(

+        {

+            "x": x.ravel(),

+            "y": y.ravel(),

+            "f(x, y)": z.ravel(),

+        }

+    )

+

+    chart = alt.Chart(data).mark_rect().encode(

+        x=alt.X("x:Q", title="$x$"),

+        y=alt.Y("y:Q", title="$y$"),

+        color=alt.Color(

+            "f(x, y):Q",

+            scale=alt.Scale(scheme=style),

+            legend=alt.Legend(title="$f(x, y)$") if colorbar else None,

+        ),

+        tooltip=["x:Q", "y:Q", "f(x, y):Q"],

+    ).properties(

+        title={

+            "text": title,

+            "subtitle": subtitle,

+            "anchor": "start",

+        },

+        width=900,

+        height=500,

+    )

+

+    chart.show()

+```
